 "5-Mod_pH", "56063831": "5-Mod_pH", "56063834": "5-Mod_pH", "56063837": "5-Mod_pH", "56063840": "5-Mod_pH", "56063846": "5-Mod_pH", "56063843": "5-Mod_pH", "56063849": "5-Mod_pH", "56063852": "5-Mod_pH", "56064377": "6-Anatomie", "56064380": "6-Anatomie", "56064383": "6-Anatomie", "56064386": "6-Anatomie", "56064389": "6-Anatomie", "56064392": "6-Anatomie", "56064395": "6-Anatomie", "56064398": "6-Anatomie", "56064401": "6-Anatomie", "56064404": "6-Anatomie", "56064407": "6-Anatomie"}, "authors": [{"id": 21405332, "full_name": "Nicolas Wanaverbecq", "first_name": "Nicolas", "last_name": "Wanaverbecq", "is_active": true, "url_name": "Nicolas_Wanaverbecq", "orcid_id": ""}, {"id": 342212, "full_name": "J\u00e9r\u00f4me Trouslard", "first_name": "J\u00e9r\u00f4me", "last_name": "Trouslard", "is_active": false, "url_name": "_", "orcid_id": ""}, {"id": 21619257, "full_name": "Riad Seddik", "first_name": "Riad", "last_name": "Seddik", "is_active": false, "url_name": "_", "orcid_id": "0000-0001-5052-4767"}, {"id": 793888, "full_name": "Caroline Michelle", "first_name": "Caroline", "last_name": "Michelle", "is_active": false, "url_name": "_", "orcid_id": ""}, {"id": 21619258, "full_name": "Jorge Ramirez-Franco", "first_name": "Jorge", "last_name": "Ramirez-Franco", "is_active": false, "url_name": "_", "orcid_id": "0000-0002-2426-6140"}, {"id": 21619269, "full_name": "Nina Jurcic", "first_name": "Nina", "last_name": "Jurcic", "is_active": false, "url_name": "_", "orcid_id": "0000-0002-5076-9753"}], "custom_fields": [], "figshare_url": "https://figshare.com/articles/dataset/Anatomical_analysis_of_CSF-contacting_distribution_in_the_mouse_spinal_cord/29430869", "download_disabled": false, "description": "<p dir=\"ltr\">Cerebrospinal Fluid-contacting neurons (CSF-cNs) are GABAergic bipolar neurons found, in contact with the cerebrospinal fluid, along the vertebrate medullo-spinal central canal. They express Polycystin Kidney Disease 2-Like 1 channels (PKD2L1), members of the Transient Receptor Potential superfamily, and were shown to modulate motor activity and therefore suggested to act as a novel sensory system. However, in mice, they remain largely uncharacterized and it is crucial to comprehensively characterize their morphological and electrophysiological properties to determine whether they form a homogenous neuronal population and understand their role in the CNS.</p><p dir=\"ltr\">We show that CSF-cNs are distributed throughout the spinal cord with a uniform morphology and a primarily ventral localization. They exhibit region-specific properties, expression of voltage-dependent and ligand-gated conductances and detect variation in extracellular pH through activation of PKD2L1 and Acid-sensing Ion Channels. They possess GABA<sub>B</sub> and muscarinic receptors, not glutamatergic metabotropic ones, to modulate Ca<sup>2+</sup> channels.</p><p dir=\"ltr\">CSF-cNs represent unique sensory neurons with a uniform morphology and electrophysiological properties that appear specific to the spinal cord segment inserted in. The future challenges in the field, will be to elucidate the physiological stimuli activating CSF-cNs and the neuronal network they are integrated in to modulate body function through specific local spinal network.</p>", "funding": "ANR-PRCI-MOTAC80C/A134/AN16HRJ NMF, ANR-PRC-MOTOX80C/213Z/AN20HRJNMF & ANR-PRC-SYMPA80C/213Z/AN21HRJNMF", "funding_list": [{"id": 29125355, "title": "ANR-PRCI-MOTAC80C/A134/AN16HRJ NMF, ANR-PRC-MOTOX80C/213Z/AN20HRJNMF & ANR-PRC-SYMPA80C/213Z/AN21HRJNMF", "grant_code": "", "funder_name": null, "is_user_defined": 1}], "version": 2, "status": "public", "size": 1848613, "created_date": "2025-07-08T13:35:34Z", "modified_date": "2025-07-08T13:35:52Z", "is_public": true, "is_confidential": false, "is_metadata_record": false, "confidential_reason": "", "metadata_reason": "", "license": {"value": 1, "name": "CC BY 4.0", "url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"}, "tags": ["Spinal cord", "PKD2L1", "central canal", "neurons", "cerebrospinal fluid", "ionotropic receptors", "metabotropic receptors", "ionic channel", "ASICs", "neurotransmitters"], "keywords": ["Spinal cord", "PKD2L1", "central canal", "neurons", "cerebrospinal fluid", "ionotropic receptors", "metabotropic receptors", "ionic channel", "ASICs", "neurotransmitters"], "categories": [{"id": 24454, "title": "Other biological sciences not elsewhere classified", "parent_id": 24445, "path": "/24130/24445/24454", "source_id": "319999", "taxonomy_id": 100}], "references": [], "has_linked_file": false, "citation": "Wanaverbecq, Nicolas; Trouslard, J\u00e9r\u00f4me; Seddik, Riad; Michelle, Caroline; Ramirez-Franco, Jorge; Jurcic, Nina (2025). Anatomical and electrophysiological analysis of CSF-contacting properties in the mouse spinal cord. figshare. Dataset. https://doi.org/10.6084/m9.figshare.29430869.v2", "related_materials": [], "is_embargoed": false, "embargo_date": null, "embargo_type": null, "embargo_title": "", "embargo_reason": "", "embargo_options": [], "id": 29430869, "title": "Anatomical and electrophysiological analysis of CSF-contacting properties in the mouse spinal cord", "doi": "10.6084/m9.figshare.29430869.v2", "handle": "", "url": "https://api.figshare.com/v2/articles/29430869", "published_date": "2025-07-08T13:35:34Z", "thumb": "", "defined_type": 3, "defined_type_name": "dataset", "group_id": null, "url_private_api": "https://api.figshare.com/v2/account/articles/29430869", "url_public_api": "https://api.figshare.com/v2/articles/29430869", "url_private_html": "https://figshare.com/account/articles/29430869", "timeline": {"posted": "2025-07-08T13:35:34", "firstOnline": "2025-07-08"}, "resource_title": null, "resource_doi": null}